linkup.snippet:
Figure 1 illustrates a traditional space data transmission architecture. In this configuration, payload-derived low-rate data streams are aggregated by a multiplexer into a consolidated high-rate signal, which is subsequently converted to an intermediate frequency (IF) signal via a high-speed Digital-to-Analog Converter (DAC).
The DPU not only implements fundamental interface data transmission functions such as TLK2711 high-speed signal communication with payloads and RS422 communication, but also incorporates a 12Tbit high-capacity storage system for retaining valid scientific payload data. To achieve this storage capacity, we selected 512 Gbit NAND FLASH memory chips.
By connecting 24 chips in parallel, this design constructs a storage array with 128-bit data width and total capacity of 12Tbit. The EMU primarily consists of an encoding/modulation FPGA, high-speed DAC, high-speed serial bus interface, and power modules. Its core functionalities include: Receiving X-band transmission data from the data processing unit via TLK2711 interfaces;
